WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.440
When you're managing a codebase with multiple distinct layers,

2
00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:09.199
directory-scoped clod.md files are how you scope rules precisely to each layer.

3
00:00:09.199 --> 00:00:12.479
For the next few minutes, we'll look at how these files

4
00:00:12.479 --> 00:00:18.159
lazy load when clod reads inside a folder, how they interact with the parent configuration,

5
00:00:18.159 --> 00:00:23.120
and practical use cases for directories like frontend, tests, and infrastructure.

6
00:00:24.479 --> 00:00:28.639
A repository with a frontend, a test suite, and a pile of terraform

7
00:00:28.639 --> 00:00:35.520
doesn't want a single set of rules, it wants several. A subdirectory config is a clod.md file

8
00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:42.400
placed inside a specific subfolder rather than the project root. Clod code lazy loads it when

9
00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:47.840
it reads a file inside that directory. No registration step, no manifest entry.

10
00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:54.319
That's what we mean by a directory-scoped clod.md instructions are scoped to a directory by virtue

11
00:00:54.319 --> 00:01:00.639
of where the file lives, so the file location is the scope boundary. And the clod.md hierarchy

12
00:01:00.639 --> 00:01:07.360
describes how project level and subdirectory configs interact. Subdirectory files add

13
00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:13.519
rules, they don't erase or replace the parent instructions. That augment rather than replace

14
00:01:13.519 --> 00:01:19.599
behavior is the single most misunderstood thing about scoped configuration, so hold on to it.

15
00:01:20.559 --> 00:01:26.959
Placement sets the scope, but the trigger is a read. Drop a clod.md file inside any subfolder,

16
00:01:26.959 --> 00:01:33.199
and clod code lazy loads it the moment it reads a file inside that directory. This is the subtle

17
00:01:33.199 --> 00:01:38.559
part worth getting right. Simply being in the folder or having it in your path isn't what

18
00:01:38.559 --> 00:01:44.720
activates the rules. The read is, until clod actually reads a file in that subdirectory,

19
00:01:44.959 --> 00:01:50.879
the subdirectory clod.md isn't loaded. So if you read a file in the front-end folder,

20
00:01:50.879 --> 00:01:56.800
the front-end clod.md loads on demand. If you then read a file in the tests folder,

21
00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:04.239
the tests clod.md loads. There's no registration step and no manifest. This makes scoping intuitive

22
00:02:04.239 --> 00:02:10.479
and easy to audit. You know which rules can apply by looking at where the clod.md files live,

23
00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:13.839
and they come into effect as clod reads into those directories.

24
00:02:15.039 --> 00:02:20.160
The relationship between a subdirectory config and the parent config is additive,

25
00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:26.479
not replacement. When clod code is working inside a subdirectory, the parent instructions are still

26
00:02:26.479 --> 00:02:33.279
active. The subdirectory file adds folder-specific rules on top of that baseline. Parent rules

27
00:02:33.279 --> 00:02:38.720
remain in effect, and since the files are concatenated root to leaf, the subdirectory

28
00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:44.960
file is simply read last. There's no guaranteed override winner, so it's best not to write direct

29
00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:51.600
contradictions at all. Think of it as layering. Each level of the directory tree adds rules to

30
00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:58.399
the stack. The root config provides the baseline everyone inherits. Subdirectory configs narrow

31
00:02:58.399 --> 00:03:04.160
and extend that baseline for specific contexts. The question people get wrong is whether a

32
00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:09.839
subdirectory clod.md cancels the parent config. It doesn't. It adds to it.

33
00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:18.000
Four directory patterns show how scoped config works in practice. A slash frontend clod.md

34
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:23.520
holds the react hooks rules, the pascal case component naming, and the import ordering

35
00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:28.320
that only matter inside that framework. None of it is meaningful anywhere else,

36
00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:32.880
and in the root file it would be noise for everyone working. Outside the frontend,

37
00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:39.119
a slash tests clod.md holds pytest fixture patterns, test naming conventions, and the

38
00:03:39.119 --> 00:03:45.919
coverage thresholds the suite enforces. A slash infrastructure clod.md carries the constraints

39
00:03:45.919 --> 00:03:51.919
that protect live cloud resources, which we'll open up next, and a slash generated clod.md

40
00:03:51.919 --> 00:03:57.759
contains a single directive. Never modify these files because they are regenerated and any edit

41
00:03:57.759 --> 00:04:02.880
is overwritten on the next run. Precision without pollution is the whole design goal.

42
00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:09.440
The infrastructure directory pattern carries higher stakes than most. Terraform manages live

43
00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:15.039
cloud resources. A wrong edit can delete things that are hard to recover. The infrastructure

44
00:04:15.039 --> 00:04:21.839
clod.md is the right place for explicit never delete rules about specific resource types,

45
00:04:21.839 --> 00:04:26.160
naming conventions that are required to be followed to keep automation working,

46
00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:32.320
and state file handling instructions that differ from normal code. Placing these rules close to

47
00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:37.839
the code they protect has a practical benefit. Anyone reviewing the infrastructure directory

48
00:04:37.839 --> 00:04:42.559
sees the constraints immediately. They don't have to remember that the root config

49
00:04:42.559 --> 00:04:47.440
has special rules for this folder. The constraints live where the risk lives.

50
00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:55.119
Root and subdirectory configs serve different purposes. The root clod.md applies across the

51
00:04:55.119 --> 00:05:00.959
entire project. It holds baseline style rules, global security constraints, and cross-cutting

52
00:05:00.959 --> 00:05:07.760
conventions that every directory inherits. A subdirectory clod.md applies only within that

53
00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:14.320
folder. It holds framework-specific rules, local conventions, and constraints tied to that code's

54
00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:19.920
specific purpose. The layering means both are active simultaneously when you're inside a

55
00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:27.760
subdirectory. Root rules set the floor. Subdirectory rules add specificity. If you need a rule to apply

56
00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:33.920
everywhere, it belongs in the root config. If a rule only makes sense in a specific context,

57
00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:40.959
it belongs in that directory's config. Verifying that scoped rules fire correctly is a real part

58
00:05:40.959 --> 00:05:46.959
of setting up subdirectory configuration. You want to confirm that working inside each subdirectory

59
00:05:46.959 --> 00:05:52.640
actually activates the folder-specific rules you placed there. You also want to confirm that

60
00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:57.839
parent rules are still active, not silently dropped. And if you've written any explicit

61
00:05:57.839 --> 00:06:03.440
contradictions between a subdirectory config and the parent config, you want to verify they resolve

62
00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:08.559
the way you intended. Scoped configuration is only reliable if it's been validated.

63
00:06:08.559 --> 00:06:15.519
A clod.md you've never tested in context might not be loading at all, or might be overriding

64
00:06:15.519 --> 00:06:22.959
something you didn't mean to override. Test it explicitly. Where you put a clod.md file is the

65
00:06:22.959 --> 00:06:29.519
whole scoping decision. That matters because a repo with a front end, a test suite, and terraform

66
00:06:29.519 --> 00:06:35.119
doesn't want one shared rulebook. Keep in mind that these files stack onto the parent instead

67
00:06:35.119 --> 00:06:41.760
of replacing it, so a local rule never quietly cancels a project-wide rule. That wraps up this

68
00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:44.000
one. I'll see you in the next lesson.

